{"name":"Salvia Blue","hex":"#96bfe6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"salvia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant in the genus Salvia, such as sage.\n\nSalvia divinorum, a plant used for its hallucinogenic properties; diviner's sage.","Salvia divinorum, a plant used for its hallucinogenic properties; diviner's sage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/salvia"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Harpendyreus notoba","blocks":[{"text":"Harpendyreus notoba, the salvia blue, is a butterfly of the family Lycaenidae. It is found in South Africa, from the northern East Cape, through the southern part of the Orange Free State to southern Gauteng the southern North West Province. It is also present in the North Cape."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harpendyreus_notoba"},{"title":"Salvia","blocks":[{"text":"Salvia is the largest genus of plants in the sage family Lamiaceae, with just under 1,000 species of shrubs, herbaceous perennials, and annuals. Within the Lamiaceae, Salvia is part of the tribe Mentheae within the subfamily Nepetoideae. One of several genera commonly referred to as sage, it includes two widely used herbs, Salvia officinalis and Salvia rosmarinus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Salvia_officinalis0.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvia"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Salvia_tingitana%2C_in_Behbahan.jpg/500px-Salvia_tingitana%2C_in_Behbahan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Salvia_tingitana%2C_in_Behbahan.jpg/1280px-Salvia_tingitana%2C_in_Behbahan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Salvia_tingitana%2C_in_Behbahan.jpg/500px-Salvia_tingitana%2C_in_Behbahan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Salvia tingitana flower in Behbahan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalvia_tingitana%2C_in_Behbahan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Anthophora_on_Salvia_1.jpg/500px-Anthophora_on_Salvia_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Anthophora_on_Salvia_1.jpg/1280px-Anthophora_on_Salvia_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src"